uality, knowledge, and expertise of the authors shines through. … The present volume is almost startlingly up-to-date ...\" (A. van der Poorten, Gazette, Australian Math. Soc. 34 (1), 2007)\nL97: ### Similar content being viewed by others\nL98: \nL99: cite19†Image†media.springernature.com L100: \nL101: ### cite20†On common index divisors and not monogenity of nonic number fields defined by trinomials of type \\(x^9+ax+b\\) L102: \nL103: Article 23 April 2024\nL104: \nL105: cite21†Image†media.springernature.com L106: \nL107: ### cite22†Number Theory L108: \nL109: Chapter © 2025\nL110: \nL111: cite23†Image†media.springernature.com L112: \nL113: ### cite24†Primality, Equations, Congruences L114: \nL115: Chapter © 2024\nL116: \nL117: Search within this book\nL118: \nL119: [Input] Search [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL120: ## Table of contents (10 chapters)\nL121: \nL122:   1. ### Front Matter\nL123: \nL124: Pages I-6\nL125: \nL126: cite25†Download chapter PDF L127: \nL128:   2. ### Problems and Tricks\nL129: \nL130:     1. #### Front Matter\nL131: \nL132: Pages 7-7\nL133: \nL134: cite26†Download chapter PDF L135: \nL136:     2. #### cite27†Number Theory L137: \nL138:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL139: \nL140: Pages 9-61\nL141: \nL142:     3. #### cite28†Some Applications of Elementary Number Theory L143: \nL144:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL145: \nL146: Pages 63-91\nL147: \nL148:   3. ### Ideas and Theories\nL149: \nL150:     1. #### Front Matter\nL151: \nL152: Pages 93-93\nL153: cite29†Download chapter PDF L154: \nL155:     2. #### cite30†Induction and Recursion L156: \nL157:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL158: \nL159: Pages 95-114\nL160: \nL161:     3. #### cite31†Arithmetic of algebraic numbers L162: \nL163:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL164: \nL165: Pages 115-189\nL166: \nL167:     4. #### cite32†Arithmetic of algebraic varieties L168: \nL169:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL170: \nL171: Pages 191-259\nL172: \nL173:     5. #### cite33†Zeta Functions and Modular Forms L174: \nL175:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL176: \nL177: Pages 261-340\nL178:     6. #### cite34†Fermat’s Last Theorem and Families of Modular Forms L179: \nL180:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL181: \nL182: Pages 341-393\nL183: \nL184:   4. ### Analogies and Visions\nL185: \nL186:     1. #### Front Matter\nL187: \nL188: Pages 393-393\nL189: \nL190: cite35†Download chapter PDF L191: \nL192:     2. #### cite36†Introductory survey to part III: motivations and description L193: \nL194:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL195: \nL196: Pages 397-413\nL197: \nL198:     3. #### cite37†Arakelov Geometry and Noncommutative Geometry (d’après C. Consani and M. Marcolli, [CM]) L199:        * Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL200: \nL201: Pages 415-460\nL202: \nL203:     4. #### cite38†Arakelov Geometry and Noncommutative Geometry (d’après C. Consani and M. Marcolli, [CM]) L204: \nL205: Pages 415-460\nL206: \nL207:   5. ### Back Matter\nL208: \nL209: Pages 461-514\nL210: \nL211: cite39†Download chapter PDF L212: \nL213: cite40†Back to top L214: ## Reviews\nL215: \nL216: From the reviews of the second edition:\nL217: \nL218: \"Here is a welcome update to Number theory I. Introduction to number theory by the same authors … . the book now brings the reader up to date with some of the latest results in the field. … The book is generally well-written and should be of interest to both the general, non-specialist reader of Number Theory as well as established researchers who are seeking an overview of some of the latest developments in the field.\"\nL219: Philip Maynard, The Mathematical Gazette, Vol. 90 (519), 2006\nL220: \nL221: [...] the first edition was a very good book; this edition is even better.\nL222: \nL223: [...] Embedded in the text are a lot of interesting ideas, insights, and clues to how the authors think about the subject. [...]\nL224: Things get more interesting in Part II (by far the largest of the tree parts)[...] This part of the book covers such things as approaches through logic, algebraic number theory, arithmetic of algebraic varieties, zeta functions, and modular forms, followed by an extensive (50+ pages ) account of Wiles' proof of Fermat's Last Theorem. This is a valuable addition, new in this edition, and serves as a vivid example of the power of the \"ideas and theories\" that dominate this part of the book.\nL225: Also new and very interesting is Part III, entitled \"Analogies and Visions,\"\nL226: \nL227: [...] The best surveys of mathematics are those written by deeply insightful mathematicians who are not afraid to infuse their ideas and insights  into their outline of subject. This is what we have here, and the result is an essential book. I only wish the price were lower so that I could encourage my students buy themselves a copy. Maybe I'll do that anyway.\nL228: \nL229: Fernado Q. Gouvêa, on 09/10/2005\nL230: \"This book is a revised and updated version of the first English translation. … Overall, the book is very well written, and has an impressive reference list. It is an excellent resource for thosewho are looking for both deep and wide understanding of number theory.\" (Alexander A. Borisov, Mathematical Reviews, Issue 2006 j)\nL231: \"This edition feels altogether different from the earlier one … . There is much new and more in this edition than in the 1995 edition: namely, one hundred and fifty extra pages. … For my part, I come to praise this fine volume. This book is a highly instructive read with the usual reminder that there lots of facts one does not know … . the quality, knowledge, and expertise of the authors shines through.\nL232: … The present volume is almost startlingly up-to-date … .\" (Alf van der Poorten, Gazette of the Australian Mathematical Society, Vol. 34 (1), 2007)\nL233: ## Authors and affiliations\nL234: \nL235:   * ### Max-Planck-Institut für Mathematik, Bonn, Germany\nL236: \nL237: Yuri Ivanovic Manin\nL238: \nL239:   * ### Institut Fourier, Université Joseph Fourier UMR 5582, Saint Martin d’Hères, France\nL240: \nL241: Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL242: \nL243: ## Accessibility information\nL244: \nL245: Accessibility information for this book is coming soon. We're working to make it available as quickly as possible. Thank you for your patience.\nL246: ## Bibliographic information\nL247: \nL248:   * Book title: Introduction to Modern Number Theory\nL249: \nL250:   * Book subtitle: Fundamental Problems, Ideas and Theories\nL251: \nL252:   * Authors: Yuri Ivanovic Manin, Alexei A. Panchishkin\nL253: \nL254:   * Series title: cite13†Encyclopaedia of Mathematical Sciences L255: \nL256:   * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-27692-0\nL257: \nL258:   * Publisher: Springer Berlin, Heidelberg\nL259: \nL260:   * eBook packages: cite41†Mathematics and Statistics , cite42†Mathematics and Statistics (R0) L261:   * Copyright information: Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2005\nL262: \nL263:   * Hardcover ISBN: 978-3-540-20364-3 Published: 23 May 2005\nL264: \nL265:   * Softcover ISBN: 978-3-642-05797-7 Published: 19 October 2010\nL266: \nL267:   * eBook ISBN: 978-3-540-27692-0 Published: 30 March 2006\nL268: \nL269:   * Series ISSN: 0938-0396\nL270: \nL271:   * Edition number: 2\nL272: \nL273:   * Number of pages: XVI, 514\nL274: \nL275:   * Additional information: Original Russian version of the first edition was published by VINITI, Moscow in 1990\nL276:   * Topics: cite43†Number Theory , cite44†Algebraic Geometry , cite45†Mathematical Logic and Foundations , cite46†Mathematical Methods in Physics , cite47†Cryptology , cite48†Numerical and Computational Physics, Simulation L277: ## Keywords\nL278: \nL279:   * cite49†Arithmetic L280:   * cite50†Arithmetic der algebr